<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  12.2 Vectors

  Requires in <docinfo>:
    <latex-image-preamble>
      \usepackage{tikz, pgfplots}
      \pgfplotsset{compat=1.18}
    </latex-image-preamble>
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Vectors</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Explain the difference between a scalar quantity and a vector quantity,
and describe a vector by its initial and terminal points.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Write a vector in component form, find the standard position vector of
<m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m>, and compute the length <m>\|\mathbf v\|</m> of a vector.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Add vectors and multiply them by scalars, both algebraically and
geometrically using the parallelogram law.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Write a vector as a linear combination of the standard unit vectors
<m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m>, and <m>\hat{k}</m>, and find the
unit vector in the direction of a given vector.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-vec-definitions">
<title>Definitions, Terminology, and Notation</title>

<p>
Some quantities can be described using real numbers alone, such as time
or temperature. To describe other quantities we need a direction as well
as a magnitude. Consider the velocity of a car: we need to know both the
speed of the car and its direction of motion to specify its velocity
completely. For instance, we might say that the car is moving with speed
<m>65</m> miles per hour in the direction of north. Such quantities are
referred to as <term>vector quantities</term>, and they are the subject
of this section.
</p>

<p>
A <term>vector</term> is a directed line segment from an
<term>initial point</term> <m>A</m> to a <term>terminal point</term>
<m>B</m>, and is denoted by <m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>. We denote the length of a
vector by <m>\|\overrightarrow{AB}\|</m>. See <xref ref="fig-vec-directed-segment"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-directed-segment">
<caption>
A vector is a directed line segment. It carries two pieces of
information: a direction, shown by the arrowhead, and a magnitude, which
is its length <m>\|\overrightarrow{AB}\|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-segment">
<diagram dimensions="(360,190)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.4,-0.4,4.4,2.4)">
<vector at="ds-v" v="(3.1,1.55)" tail="(0.55,0.35)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<point at="ds-A" p="(0.55,0.35)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<point at="ds-B" p="(3.65,1.90)" fill="#0072B2"/>
<label at="ds-A-lab" anchor="(0.5,0.28)" alignment="sw" color="#0072B2">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="ds-B-lab" anchor="(3.72,1.94)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
<m>B</m>
</label>
<label at="ds-init" anchor="(0.72,0.2)" alignment="se">
<m>\text{initial point}</m>
</label>
<label at="ds-term" anchor="(3.62,2.04)" alignment="nw">
<m>\text{terminal point}</m>
</label>
<label at="ds-name" anchor="(2.0,1.28)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>\overrightarrow{AB}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A single blue arrow points up and to the right from a dot labelled A, the initial point, to a dot labelled B, the terminal point. The arrow is labelled vector A B.">
  <annotation ref="ds-A" text="The initial point A, where the vector begins."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-B" text="The terminal point B, where the vector ends and the arrowhead is drawn."/>
  <annotation ref="ds-v" text="The vector from A to B, written vector A B. Its length is the magnitude of the vector."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Two vectors are <term>equal</term> if they have the same direction and
the same magnitude. Notice that this definition says nothing about where
a vector is located: a vector may be moved around the plane freely, and
as long as its direction and length are unchanged it is the same vector.
The vectors shown in <xref ref="fig-vec-equal"/> are all equal to each
other, <m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{CD} = \overrightarrow{OP} = \overrightarrow{EF}</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-equal">
<caption>
Four equal vectors. They have different initial points, but the same
direction and the same length, so
<m>\overrightarrow{AB} = \overrightarrow{CD} = \overrightarrow{OP} = \overrightarrow{EF}</m>. Only <m>\overrightarrow{OP}</m>
begins at the origin.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-equal">
<diagram dimensions="(390,300)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.6,3.6,3.4)">
<axes at="eq-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<vector at="eq-AB" v="(1.9,0.95)" tail="(-2.3,1.55)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="eq-CD" v="(1.9,0.95)" tail="(-0.55,0.35)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="eq-OP" v="(1.9,0.95)" tail="(0,0)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="eq-EF" v="(1.9,0.95)" tail="(-2.5,-1.75)"
        stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<label at="eq-A" anchor="(-2.38,1.5)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>A</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-B" anchor="(-0.32,2.56)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>B</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-C" anchor="(-0.63,0.3)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>C</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-D" anchor="(1.42,1.36)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>D</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-O" anchor="(-0.08,-0.05)" alignment="sw">
<m>O</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-P" anchor="(1.97,1.02)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>P</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-E" anchor="(-2.58,-1.8)" alignment="w" color="#0072B2">
<m>E</m>
</label>
<label at="eq-F" anchor="(-0.52,-0.74)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>F</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Four arrows of the same length all pointing up and to the right at the same angle, placed at four different positions in the coordinate plane. Three are blue, from A to B, from C to D, and from E to F. The fourth, drawn in orange from the origin O to the point P, is the one in standard position.">
  <annotation ref="eq-AB" text="The vector from A to B."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-CD" text="The vector from C to D, equal to vector A B because it has the same direction and length."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-OP" text="The vector from the origin O to the point P, drawn in orange. This is the one in standard position."/>
  <annotation ref="eq-EF" text="The vector from E to F, also equal to the others."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Only one of the vectors in <xref ref="fig-vec-equal"/> starts from the
origin. We say that <m>\overrightarrow{OP}</m> is in <term>standard position</term>,
and we denote it by a bold letter such as
<m>\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{OP}</m>, which is also referred to as the
<term>standard position vector</term>.
</p>

<p>
In 2D, if the initial point of a vector is the origin, i.e. <m>(0,0)</m>,
and its final point is <m>(v_1,v_2)</m>, then the
<term>component form</term> of the vector is
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2 \rangle</m>. In 3D, if the initial point
of a vector is the origin, i.e. <m>(0,0,0)</m>, and its final point is
<m>(v_1,v_2,v_3)</m>, then the component form of the vector is
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3 \rangle</m>.
</p>

<p>
In <xref ref="fig-vec-components"/> we have <m>\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{PQ}</m>.
Also <m>x_1 + v_1 = x_2</m>, <m>y_1 + v_2 = y_2</m>, and
<m>z_1 + v_3 = z_2</m>. This means we have
<md xml:id="eq-vec-components" number="yes">
\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3 \rangle
= \langle x_2 - x_1,\; y_2 - y_1,\; z_2 - z_1 \rangle.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-components">
<caption>
The vector <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m> and its standard position vector
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3 \rangle</m>. The two arrows are
equal as vectors; only the second one begins at the origin.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system. A blue arrow runs from the point P
to the point Q. A second arrow of the same length and direction, drawn in
magenta, runs from the origin to the point with coordinates v one, v two,
v three. Dashed lines drop from the tip of the magenta arrow to show its
three components along the axes.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.92, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (4.0,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,5.0,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.8) node[above] {$z$};
\node[above left] at (0,0,0) {$0$};
% PQ, mirrored across the y-axis (x -> -x), z kept positive
\coordinate (P) at (-2.6,1.0,1.1);
\coordinate (Q) at (-0.9,3.4,2.6);
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (P) -- (Q);
\fill[cyan!60!black] (P) circle (1.6pt);
\fill[cyan!60!black] (Q) circle (1.6pt);
\node[cyan!60!black, below=2pt] at (P) {$P(x_1,y_1,z_1)$};
\node[cyan!60!black, above right=1pt] at (Q) {$Q(x_2,y_2,z_2)$};
% standard position vector v = Q - P
\coordinate (V) at (1.7,2.4,1.5);
\draw[->, magenta, very thick] (0,0,0) -- (V);
\fill[magenta] (V) circle (1.6pt);
\node[magenta, right=1pt] at (V) {$\mathbf v=\langle v_1,v_2,v_3\rangle$};
% components, dashed, with each label attached next to its own segment
\draw[magenta, dashed] (0,0,0) -- (1.7,0,0)
                                -- (1.7,2.4,0) node[pos=0.5, below] {$v_2$}
                                -- (V);
\draw[magenta, dashed] (1.7,2.4,0) -- (0,2.4,0) node[pos=0.5, below] {$v_1$};
\node[magenta, right] at (1.7,2.4,0.75) {$v_3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The magnitude of <m>\mathbf v = \overrightarrow{PQ}</m> is the same as the distance
between the two points <m>P(x_1,y_1,z_1)</m> and <m>Q(x_2,y_2,z_2)</m>,
that is
<md>
<mrow>\|\mathbf v\| = \|\overrightarrow{PQ}\| \amp = \sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + v_3^2}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-vec-length" number="yes">\amp = \sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2 + (y_1-y_2)^2 + (z_1-z_2)^2}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<example xml:id="example-vec-length">
<title>Length and Standard Position Vector</title>
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Compute the length of the vector with the initial point <m>P(-1,0,1)</m>
and the final point <m>Q(1,1,-1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the standard position vector corresponding to this vector.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> By <xref ref="eq-vec-length"/>, the length of the
vector <m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m> is
<md>
<mrow>\|\overrightarrow{PQ}\| \amp = \sqrt{(x_1-x_2)^2 + (y_1-y_2)^2 + (z_1-z_2)^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \sqrt{(-1-1)^2 + (0-1)^2 + (1+1)^2} = 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> By <xref ref="eq-vec-components"/>, the standard
position vector is
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf v \amp = \langle x_2 - x_1,\; y_2 - y_1,\; z_2 - z_1 \rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \langle 1-(-1),\; 1-0,\; -1-1 \rangle = \langle 2, 1, -2 \rangle.</mrow>
</md>
As a check, <m>\|\mathbf v\| = \sqrt{2^2 + 1^2 + (-2)^2} = 3</m>, which
agrees with part <alert>A</alert>, as it must, since <m>\mathbf v</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{PQ}</m> are the same vector.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-vec-algebra">
<title>Vector Algebra Operations</title>

<definition xml:id="def-vec-addition">
<title>Vector Addition</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3 \rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3 \rangle</m>. The <term>sum</term>
of <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-vec-addition" number="yes">
\mathbf u + \mathbf v
= \langle u_1+v_1,\; u_2+v_2,\; u_3+v_3 \rangle.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
<term>Parallelogram law.</term> The parallelogram law tells us how to add
two vectors geometrically, and is described in
<xref ref="fig-vec-parallelogram"/>. Placing the tail of <m>\mathbf v</m>
at the head of <m>\mathbf u</m> produces the sum as the third side of a
triangle; equivalently, drawing both vectors from a common initial point
makes the sum the diagonal of the parallelogram they span.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-parallelogram">
<caption>
The parallelogram law. On the left, the components add head to tail. On
the right, <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> is the diagonal of the
parallelogram spanned by <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-parallelogram">
<diagram dimensions="(620,270)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.55,-0.55,9.9,3.9)">
<!-- = left panel: head to tail = -->
<vector at="pl-xaxis" v="(4.2,0)" tail="(-0.2,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pl-yaxis" v="(0,3.5)" tail="(0,-0.2)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pl-u" v="(2.1,0.85)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pl-v" v="(1.15,1.75)" tail="(2.1,0.85)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pl-sum" v="(3.25,2.6)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="pl-d1" endpoints="((2.1,0.85),(3.25,0.85))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="4 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pl-d2" endpoints="((3.25,0.85),(3.25,2.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="4 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pl-d3" endpoints="((2.1,0),(2.1,0.85))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="4 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="pl-x" anchor="(4.0,-0.06)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pl-y" anchor="(-0.06,3.35)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="pl-u-lab" anchor="(1.0,0.5)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="pl-v-lab" anchor="(2.75,1.65)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="pl-sum-lab" anchor="(1.35,1.72)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="pl-u1" anchor="(1.05,-0.08)" alignment="s"><m>u_1</m></label>
<label at="pl-u2" anchor="(2.02,0.42)" alignment="w"><m>u_2</m></label>
<label at="pl-v1" anchor="(2.68,0.78)" alignment="s"><m>v_1</m></label>
<label at="pl-v2" anchor="(3.32,1.72)" alignment="e"><m>v_2</m></label>
<label at="pl-top" anchor="(3.2,2.72)" alignment="nw">
<m>\langle u_1+v_1,\; u_2+v_2\rangle</m>
</label>
<!-- = right panel: the parallelogram = -->
<vector at="pr-xaxis" v="(4.2,0)" tail="(5.4,0)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pr-yaxis" v="(0,3.5)" tail="(5.6,-0.2)" stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pr-u" v="(2.1,0.85)" tail="(5.6,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pr-v" v="(1.15,1.75)" tail="(5.6,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="pr-sum" v="(3.25,2.6)" tail="(5.6,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="pr-p1" endpoints="((7.7,0.85),(8.85,2.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="4 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pr-p2" endpoints="((6.75,1.75),(8.85,2.6))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="4 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="pr-x" anchor="(9.4,-0.06)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pr-y" anchor="(5.54,3.35)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
<label at="pr-u-lab" anchor="(6.75,0.35)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="pr-v-lab" anchor="(6.05,1.35)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="pr-sum-lab" anchor="(6.95,0.58)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two coordinate pictures side by side. On the left, the blue vector u is drawn from the origin and the blue vector v is drawn starting at the head of u; the orange sum vector u plus v runs from the origin to the head of v, and dashed lines show how the components add. On the right, u and v are both drawn from the origin and dashed lines complete a parallelogram whose diagonal is the orange vector u plus v.">
  <annotation ref="pl-u" text="The vector u, drawn from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="pl-v" text="The vector v, drawn with its tail at the head of u, head to tail."/>
  <annotation ref="pl-sum" text="The sum u plus v, reaching from the origin to the head of v. Its components are the sums of the components of u and v."/>
  <annotation ref="pr-u" text="The vector u drawn from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="pr-v" text="The vector v drawn from the same origin."/>
  <annotation ref="pr-sum" text="The sum u plus v is the diagonal of the parallelogram spanned by u and v."/>
  <annotation ref="pr-p1" text="Dashed sides completing the parallelogram."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-vec-add">
<title>Adding Two Vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
Add the following two vectors geometrically and algebraically.
<md>
\mathbf u = \langle 1,1 \rangle \quad \text{and} \quad
\mathbf v = \langle -2,1 \rangle
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Algebraically, by <xref ref="eq-vec-addition"/> we have
<md>
\mathbf u + \mathbf v = \langle 1,1 \rangle + \langle -2,1 \rangle
= \langle -1, 2 \rangle.
</md>
For the geometrical part we use the parallelogram law, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-vec-add-example"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-add-example">
<caption>
Adding <m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,1\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -2,1\rangle</m> with the parallelogram law. The
diagonal of the parallelogram is
<m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v = \langle -1,2 \rangle</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-add-example">
<diagram dimensions="(430,300)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-0.7,2.1,2.9)">
<grid-axes at="ae-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<vector at="ae-u" v="(1,1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="ae-v" v="(-2,1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="ae-sum" v="(-1,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
<line at="ae-d1" endpoints="((1,1),(-1,2))" stroke="#8B1A4A"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="ae-d2" endpoints="((-2,1),(-1,2))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="ae-u-lab" anchor="(1.05,0.86)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u=\langle 1,1\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="ae-v-lab" anchor="(-2.05,0.92)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\mathbf v=\langle -2,1\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="ae-sum-lab" anchor="(-1.06,2.08)" alignment="nw">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v=\langle -1,2\rangle</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid. The blue vector u goes from the origin to the point 1 comma 1. The dark red vector v goes from the origin to negative 2 comma 1. Dashed lines parallel to each vector complete a parallelogram, and the black vector from the origin to negative 1 comma 2 is its diagonal, the sum u plus v.">
  <annotation ref="ae-u" text="The vector u equals 1 comma 1, drawn in blue from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="ae-v" text="The vector v equals negative 2 comma 1, drawn in dark red from the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="ae-sum" text="The sum u plus v equals negative 1 comma 2, the diagonal of the parallelogram, drawn in black."/>
  <annotation ref="ae-d1" text="A dashed copy of v, drawn from the head of u, showing the head to tail construction."/>
  <annotation ref="ae-d2" text="A dashed copy of u, drawn from the head of v."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<definition xml:id="def-vec-scalar">
<title>Scalar Multiplication</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3 \rangle</m> and let
<m>k \in \R</m> be a scalar. The <term>scalar multiple</term>
<m>k\,\mathbf u</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-vec-scalar" number="yes">
k\,\mathbf u = \langle k u_1,\; k u_2,\; k u_3 \rangle.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Note that both definitions can be reduced to the 2D case simply by
removing the last components of the vectors.
</p>

<p>
Let <m>k \in \R</m> and <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1,u_2,u_3\rangle</m>.
Then the length of the vector
<m>k\mathbf u = \langle ku_1, ku_2, ku_3 \rangle</m> is
<m>|k|\,\|\mathbf u\|</m>, where <m>|k|</m> is the absolute value of
<m>k</m> and <m>\|\mathbf u\|</m> is the length of <m>\mathbf u</m>. It
is easy to see why this is the case:
<md>
<mrow>\|k\mathbf u\| \amp = \sqrt{k^2\left(u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2\right)}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-vec-scalar-length" number="yes">\amp = |k|\sqrt{u_1^2+u_2^2+u_3^2} = |k|\,\|\mathbf u\|.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
The vector <m>-\mathbf u</m> has the same length as <m>\mathbf u</m>;
however, the two vectors point in opposite directions. In general the
length of <m>k\mathbf u</m> is <m>|k|</m> times the length of
<m>\mathbf u</m>, and if <m>k \gt 0</m> the two vectors point in the same
direction, whereas if <m>k \lt 0</m> they point in opposite directions.
<xref ref="fig-vec-scalar"/> shows several examples.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-scalar">
<caption>
Scalar multiples of <m>\mathbf u</m>. Multiplying by <m>1.5</m> or
<m>2</m> stretches the vector without turning it; multiplying by
<m>-2</m> stretches it and reverses its direction.
</caption>
<image width="80%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-scalar">
<diagram dimensions="(500,210)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-1.7,9.5,2.3)">
<vector at="sc-u" v="(1.0,0.75)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="sc-15u" v="(1.5,1.125)" tail="(2.3,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="sc-2u" v="(2.0,1.5)" tail="(4.7,0)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="sc-m2u" v="(-2.0,-1.5)" tail="(9.2,0.9)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<label at="sc-u-lab" anchor="(0.45,0.42)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="sc-15u-lab" anchor="(2.95,0.65)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>1.5\,\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="sc-2u-lab" anchor="(5.6,0.85)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>2\,\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="sc-m2u-lab" anchor="(8.3,0.05)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>-2\,\mathbf u</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Four arrows in a row. The first, in blue, is the vector u. The next two, in orange, are one and a half u and two u; they point the same way as u but are longer. The last, also orange, is negative two u; it has the same length as two u but points the opposite way, down and to the left.">
  <annotation ref="sc-u" text="The original vector u, drawn in blue."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-15u" text="One and a half times u: same direction, one and a half times as long."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-2u" text="Two times u: same direction, twice as long."/>
  <annotation ref="sc-m2u" text="Negative two times u: twice as long as u but pointing in the opposite direction, because the scalar is negative."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<definition xml:id="def-vec-subtraction">
<title>Vector Subtraction</title>
<statement>
<p>
Subtraction is handled with the same idea: the <term>difference</term>
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m> means
<md xml:id="eq-vec-subtraction" number="yes">
\mathbf u - \mathbf v = \mathbf u + (-\mathbf v),
</md>
so we reverse <m>\mathbf v</m> and then add. Geometrically,
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m> is the vector that points from the head of
<m>\mathbf v</m> to the head of <m>\mathbf u</m> when both are drawn
from a common initial point.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-vec-add-subtract">
<title>Adding and Subtracting Geometrically</title>
<statement>
<p>
Given the two vectors <m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,1 \rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle -1,2 \rangle</m>, find
<m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> and <m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>
geometrically.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
For the sum we complete the parallelogram spanned by <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>. For the difference we use
<xref ref="eq-vec-subtraction"/>: we first draw
<m>-\mathbf v = \langle 1,-2 \rangle</m> and then add it to
<m>\mathbf u</m>, which gives
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v = \langle 2,-1 \rangle</m>. Both constructions
are shown in <xref ref="fig-vec-add-sub-example"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-add-sub-example">
<caption>
The sum <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v = \langle 0,3 \rangle</m> is the
diagonal of the parallelogram spanned by <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>. The difference
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v = \langle 2,-1 \rangle</m> is obtained by adding
<m>-\mathbf v</m> to <m>\mathbf u</m>; equivalently, it points from the
head of <m>\mathbf v</m> to the head of <m>\mathbf u</m>.
</caption>
<image width="76%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-vec-add-sub">
<diagram dimensions="(420,360)" margins="12">
<coordinates bbox="(-2.2,-2.6,3.2,3.5)">
<grid-axes at="as-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<vector at="as-u" v="(1,1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="as-v" v="(-1,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="as-mv" v="(1,-2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="as-sum" v="(0,3)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#E69F00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="as-diff" v="(2,-1)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
<line at="as-d1" endpoints="((1,1),(0,3))" stroke="#8B1A4A"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="as-d2" endpoints="((-1,2),(0,3))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="as-d3" endpoints="((1,1),(2,-1))" stroke="#009E73"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="as-d4" endpoints="((1,-2),(2,-1))" stroke="#0072B2"
      dash="5 4" thickness="1.5"/>
<label at="as-u-lab" anchor="(0.72,0.62)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="as-v-lab" anchor="(-0.85,1.42)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="as-mv-lab" anchor="(0.62,-1.55)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>-\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="as-sum-lab" anchor="(0.08,2.35)" alignment="e" color="#E69F00">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="as-diff-lab" anchor="(1.3,-0.72)" alignment="s">
<m>\mathbf u-\mathbf v</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate grid with several vectors from the origin. Blue u goes to 1 comma 1, dark red v goes to negative 1 comma 2, and green negative v goes to 1 comma negative 2. The amber vector u plus v goes straight up to 0 comma 3 and is the diagonal of the parallelogram formed by u and v. The black vector u minus v goes to 2 comma negative 1 and is the diagonal of the parallelogram formed by u and negative v.">
  <annotation ref="as-u" text="The vector u equals 1 comma 1."/>
  <annotation ref="as-v" text="The vector v equals negative 1 comma 2."/>
  <annotation ref="as-mv" text="The vector negative v equals 1 comma negative 2, the same length as v but reversed."/>
  <annotation ref="as-sum" text="The sum u plus v equals 0 comma 3, drawn in amber."/>
  <annotation ref="as-diff" text="The difference u minus v equals 2 comma negative 1, found by adding negative v to u."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<p>
The operations satisfy the familiar algebraic rules collected in
<xref ref="thm-vec-properties"/>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-vec-properties">
<title>Properties of Vector Operations</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf u</m>, <m>\mathbf v</m>, <m>\mathbf w</m> be vectors and
<m>a</m>, <m>b</m> be scalars. Then
<md>
<mrow>1.\;\; \mathbf u + \mathbf v \amp= \mathbf v + \mathbf u
       \amp\qquad 2.\;\; (\mathbf u + \mathbf v) + \mathbf w
       \amp= \mathbf u + (\mathbf v + \mathbf w)</mrow>
<mrow>3.\;\; \mathbf u + \mathbf 0 \amp= \mathbf u
       \amp\qquad 4.\;\; \mathbf u + (-\mathbf u) \amp= \mathbf 0</mrow>
<mrow>5.\;\; 0\,\mathbf u \amp= \mathbf 0
       \amp\qquad 6.\;\; 1\,\mathbf u \amp= \mathbf u</mrow>
<mrow>7.\;\; a(b\mathbf u) \amp= (ab)\mathbf u
       \amp\qquad 8.\;\; a(\mathbf u + \mathbf v)
       \amp= a\mathbf u + a\mathbf v</mrow>
<mrow>9.\;\; (a+b)\mathbf u \amp= a\mathbf u + b\mathbf u \amp \amp</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-vec-unit">
<title>Unit Vectors</title>

<p>
Vectors of length one are referred to as <term>unit vectors</term>. The
<term>standard unit vectors</term> are the ones pointing in the positive
direction of <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and <m>z</m>, and they are denoted by
<m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m>, and <m>\hat{k}</m> respectively, as
in <xref ref="fig-vec-ijk"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-vec-ijk">
<caption>
The standard unit vectors <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m>, and
<m>\hat{k}</m>, and the decomposition
<m>\mathbf a = a_1\hat{\imath} + a_2\hat{\jmath} + a_3\hat{k}</m> of a vector
into components along the axes.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<description>
<p>
A three dimensional coordinate system showing three short magenta arrows
of length one along the positive x, y and z axes, labelled i, j and k. A
longer magenta arrow a runs from the origin to the point with coordinates
a one, a two, a three, and blue arrows along the axes show its three
components a one i, a two j and a three k.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)},
                    scale=0.95, >=stealth]
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (4.2,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,4.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,3.6) node[above] {$z$};
% standard unit vectors, drawn thicker than the other vectors
\draw[->, magenta, line width=1.8pt] (0,0,0) -- (1,0,0);
\node[magenta, above] at (0.5,0,0) {$\hat{\imath}$};
\draw[->, magenta, line width=1.8pt] (0,0,0) -- (0,1,0) node[right] {$\hat{\jmath}$};
\draw[->, magenta, line width=1.8pt] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,1) node[left] {$\hat{k}$};
% a general vector a = (2.4, 3.2, 2.2)
\coordinate (A) at (2.4,3.2,2.2);
\draw[->, magenta, very thick] (0,0,0) -- (A);
\fill[magenta] (A) circle (1.6pt);
\node[magenta, above right=1pt] at (A) {$\langle a_1,a_2,a_3\rangle$};
\node[magenta] at (1.1,1.5,1.35) {$\mathbf a$};
% components
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (0,0,0) -- (2.4,0,0)
      node[pos=0.35, below] {$a_1\hat{\imath}$};
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (2.4,0,0) -- (2.4,3.2,0)
      node[midway, below] {$a_2\hat{\jmath}$};
\draw[->, cyan!60!black, very thick] (2.4,3.2,0) -- (A)
      node[midway, right] {$a_3\hat{k}$};
\draw[gray, dashed] (0,0,0) -- (2.4,3.2,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Combining <xref ref="eq-vec-addition"/> and <xref ref="eq-vec-scalar"/>,
any vector <m>\mathbf a = \langle a_1,a_2,a_3\rangle</m> can be written
as a linear combination of <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m>, and
<m>\hat{k}</m> as
<md xml:id="eq-vec-ijk" number="yes">
\mathbf a = \langle a_1,a_2,a_3\rangle
= a_1\hat{\imath} + a_2\hat{\jmath} + a_3\hat{k}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
To find a unit vector pointing in the same direction as a nonzero vector
<m>\mathbf a</m>, we divide <m>\mathbf a</m> by its own length. We write
<m>\hat{a}</m>, read <q>a hat</q>, for the resulting unit vector:
<md xml:id="eq-vec-unit" number="yes">
\hat{a} = \frac{\mathbf a}{\|\mathbf a\|}.
</md>
By <xref ref="eq-vec-scalar-length"/> this vector has length
<m>\frac{1}{\|\mathbf a\|}\|\mathbf a\| = 1</m>, as required. From now
on a hat always marks a vector of length one, as it does for the standard
unit vectors <m>\hat{\imath}</m>, <m>\hat{\jmath}</m>, and
<m>\hat{k}</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-vec-unit">
<title>Standard Unit Vectors and a Unit Direction</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two vectors
<m>\mathbf v = \langle 1,0,1 \rangle = \hat{\imath} + \hat{k}</m> and
<m>\mathbf u = \langle -1,1,0 \rangle = -\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Express <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> in terms of the standard unit
vectors.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the unit vector in the direction of <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> We have <m>\mathbf v = \langle 1,0,1 \rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf u = \langle -1,1,0 \rangle</m>, and hence, using
<xref ref="eq-vec-addition"/> and <xref ref="eq-vec-ijk"/>,
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf u + \mathbf v \amp= \langle 1,0,1 \rangle
      + \langle -1,1,0 \rangle = \langle 0,1,1 \rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp= (-\hat{\imath} + \hat{\jmath}) + (\hat{\imath} + \hat{k})
      = \hat{\jmath} + \hat{k}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> The length of <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> is
<m>\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\| = \sqrt{0 + 1 + 1} = \sqrt 2</m>, and hence
by <xref ref="eq-vec-unit"/> we find the unit vector by dividing
<m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> by its length:
<md>
\widehat{\mathbf u + \mathbf v}
= \frac{\mathbf u + \mathbf v}{\|\mathbf u + \mathbf v\|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}(\hat{\jmath} + \hat{k})
= \left\langle 0,\; \frac{1}{\sqrt 2},\; \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}
\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
